title: Algorithm – Matrix Math

files:
  - em.prog:em.benchmark.coremark/MatrixBench.em.ts
  - .assets/img-102-03-01.png

steps:
  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$start]&thinsp;%[cu]MatrixBench% Algorithm}

      We've arrived at the heart of **CoreMark** &ndash; its trio of bench&shy;mark algorithms and their implementation in **EMS**.

      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} <code>core_list_join.c&nbsp;</code>{[cu]ListBench&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}[&thinsp;list-processing&thinsp;]<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} <code>core_matrix.c&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</code>{[cu]MatrixBench&nbsp;&nbsp;}[&thinsp;matrix-math&thinsp;]<br>
      {[bi,arrow_circle_right]} <code>core_state.c&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</code>{[cu]StateBench&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;}[&thinsp;state-machine&thinsp;]<br>

      Like {[cu]CoreBench} seen previously, each of these modules implements a common {[cu]BenchAlgI} interface.

      This tour focuses exclusively on {[cu]MatrixBench} &ndash; perhaps the most approachable of the three **CoreMark** algorithms.

  - cmds:
      - close 2
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 8
      - mark 1 B 15
      - mark 1 C 27
    focus: [1, 1]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]MatrixBench% &ndash; %[cx]em$meta% Details}

      {[cu]MatrixBench} subjects the three {[cs]$table} objects declared at line {[bm,B]} to a variety of matrix-math operations.

      The value of {[cn]mem_size}{[bm,A]} (bound by {[cu]CoreBench} at build-time) ultimately determines the dimension of {[cn]mat&lbrack;ABC&rbrack;}.

      A calculation inside {[cs]em$construct} eventually binds this dimension value to the {[cn]dimN} config parameter at line {[bm,C]}.

      {[in]The subsequent loop then dimensions and zeroes the %[cs]$table% matrices using the intrinsic <b>%[cx]$$add%</b> operator.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 55
      - mark 1 B 67
    focus: [1, 51]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]MatrixBench% &ndash; %[cf]run% Details}

      Starting with its scalar argument, {[cu]MatrixBench}.{[cf]run} ma&shy;nip&shy;ulates {[cn]mat&lbrack;ABC&rbrack;} through a series of private functions.

      {[in]Navigate to helpers like %[cf]addVal% and %[cf]mulVec%, which match functions originally defined in %[cx]core_matrix.c%.}

      To compute its {[ct]sum_t} result, {[cu]MatrixBench}.{[cf]run} updates a local {[cn]crc} variable with a scalar returned by {[cf]sumDat}{[bm,A]}.

      The {[ct]sum_t} returned by {[cf]run} folds the accumulated {[cn]crc} value into the benchmark's {[cn]FINAL} checksum {[bm,B]}.

  - cmds:
      - open 1
      - mark 1 A 71
    focus: [1, 74]
    text: |
      {[ht,$details]&thinsp;%[cu]MatrixBench% &ndash; %[cf]setup% Details}

      The {[cu]MatrixBench}.{[cf]setup} initializes (at *run-time*) the {[cn]matA} and {[cn]matB} objects dimensioned and zeroed at *build-time*.

      The scalar values held in these matrices ultimately derive from special **CoreMark** seed values read here at line {[bm,A]}.

      {[in]Recall that %[cu]UT%.%[cf]getSeed% %[tr,102/02/03]% forcibly reads (at <i>run-time</i>) an unknown constant value bound at <i>build-time</i>.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 74]
    text: |
      {[ht,fence]&thinsp;%[cu]BenchAlgI%.%[cf]setup% &ndash; Domain Boundaries}

      The {[cf]setup} function we've just examined might appear to belong in the **`em$meta`** domain.

      {[in]Build-time initialization of complex data structures in fact distinguishes <b>EMS</b> from other embedded languages.}

      Moving **`em$targ`** code upstream to the **`em$meta`** domain reduces program size and often improves performance.

      But **CoreMark** forbids this &ndash; fearing a compiler might "pre-compute" benchmark results from static datasets.

      **CoreMark** thus requires *explicit* memory reads of all seed values &ndash; like any program input known only at run-time.

      {[in]Our <b>EMS</b> modules will maximize build-time initial&shy;ization, while still respecting <b>CoreMark</b>'s inherent constraint.}

  - cmds:
      - open 1
    focus: [1, 40]
    text: |
      {[ht,$extra]&thinsp;Extra Credit}

      {[bi,task_alt]} Find {[cu]MatrixBench} helpers inside {[cx]core_matrix.c}.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Compare helper functions to sharpen your **EMS** skills.

      {[bi,task_alt]} Call {[cu]MatrixBench}.{[cf]print} to inspect interim results.

  - cmds:
      - view 2
    text: |
      {[ht,$steps]&thinsp;Next Steps}

      One down, two to go....

      We'll now move on to {[cu]StateBench} and its implementa&shy;tion of the algorithm codified in {[cx]core_state.c}.

      {[hc]}
